<p>The design language is familiar to anyone who has used a PocketBook Era: a compact, paperback-sized body with page-turn buttons on the right bezel. The buttons are tactile and responsive, ideal for one-handed reading. The SMARTlight technology adjusts both brightness and color temperature automatically based on ambient light — warm tones for nighttime reading, cooler tones for daytime sessions.</p>

<p>The IPX4 water resistance means the Era Lite can handle splashes, rain, and accidental drops in the bath or poolside. It is not designed for full submersion, but it offers enough protection for everyday use that Kindle Paperwhite owners have come to expect.</p>

<h2 id="open-ecosystem">The Open Ecosystem: What Makes PocketBook Different from Kindle</h2>

<p>The single biggest differentiator between PocketBook and Amazon's Kindle lineup is not hardware — it is software philosophy. PocketBook builds its devices around the principle that readers should own their files, not rent access to them through a proprietary store. This philosophy manifests in several concrete ways:</p>

<p><strong>Native EPUB support.</strong> Where Kindle requires conversion via Send to Kindle or Calibre, PocketBook reads EPUB files directly. Drag an EPUB onto the device via USB, and it appears in your library, fully formatted, with working fonts, images, and layout. No emailing files to a Kindle address. No format conversion. No AZW-to-EPUB workarounds.</p>

<p><strong>Multi-store flexibility.</strong> PocketBook devices ship with access to the PocketBook Book Store (millions of titles from major publishers), but they also support Adobe DRM for library books, direct sideloading from any source, and integration with cloud services like Dropbox and PocketBook Cloud. You can buy a book from Kobo, borrow one from your local library via Overdrive, download a free classic from Project Gutenberg, and have them all sit side by side in the same library interface.</p>

<p><strong>No ecosystem lock-in.</strong> Amazon's Kindle ecosystem is designed to keep you inside it. PocketBook's is designed to let you leave at any time. Your books are files on the device that you can copy back to your computer, archive, convert, or share between devices without restrictions. This matters for long-term digital preservation — a topic that has gained urgency as Amazon retires older Kindle models and discontinues features like Download &amp; Transfer via USB.</p>

<p><strong>Wide format support.</strong> In addition to EPUB, the Era Lite handles PDF, MOBI (the old Kindle format), FB2 (popular in Eastern Europe), DJVU (for scanned documents), CBR/CBZ (comic book archives), and common office formats like DOCX, RTF, and HTML. For readers who maintain mixed-format libraries, this eliminates the need for batch conversion before moving files to the device.</p>

<h2 id="epub-handling">How the Era Lite Handles EPUB Files</h2>

<p>EPUB rendering quality is the most important factor for an open-ecosystem e-reader, and PocketBook has been refining its EPUB engine for over a decade. The Era Lite uses PocketBook's proprietary reading engine (not Adobe RMSDK by default), which offers:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Accurate CSS rendering.</strong> PocketBook's engine handles CSS Level 2.1 and most of Level 3, including flexbox-like layouts that trip up older RMSDK-based readers. This means EPUBs with complex formatting — poetry with indentation, textbooks with sidebars, cookbooks with tables — render as the publisher intended.</li>
  <li><strong>Custom font support.</strong> You can sideload any TrueType or OpenType font onto the device and apply it to any book. This is a feature Kindle added only recently (on newer models) and still restricts to supported formats.</li>
  <li><strong>Margin, line-spacing, and alignment controls.</strong> Full control over typography per book or globally, including justification options, hyphenation dictionaries for 20+ languages, and margin fine-tuning.</li>
  <li><strong>Dictionary lookup.</strong> Built-in dictionaries for multiple languages, plus Wikipedia and Wiktionary integration. Long-press any word for instant lookup — works across all EPUB files regardless of source.</li>
  <li><strong>Reading statistics.</strong> Estimated time per chapter, reading speed, and session tracking — comparable to Kindle's Reading Insight feature.</li>
</ul>

<p>The Era Lite also supports Adobe DRM for library EPUBs via Overdrive and Libby. This means you can borrow EPUB books from your public library and read them on the Era Lite just as you would on a Kobo — no additional software or configuration needed. Simply sign into your Adobe ID on the device, download the library book, and start reading.</p>

